{"type":"result","key":"v2:d2dc72e4f7b899cf665f6728a5e726720cc6e572750f01639c9d89924fb9c720","agentId":"a81651fb839e21922","result":{"lens":"Injection and code execution: every path from data this system does not control (OData HTTP response bodies, recorded fixtures, file paths outside the repo, subprocess output) to something that executes or is interpreted — subprocess, urlopen, SQL emission/execution, YAML loading, sqlfluff, and file writes.","findings":[],"consideredAndRejected":["subprocess.run in src/adss/engine.py:82 — argument list is [str(self.binary), *arguments, '--no-tui'] with no shell=True. self.binary is Project.engine_binary (a fixed repo-relative path) and *arguments is only ever the literals 'install'/'--connection'/'dev'/'deploy'/'execute' passed from src/adss/cli.py:214, 222, 231. No HTTP body, fixture value, or filename reaches an argv element. The binary is additionally sha256-verified against bin/linux-amd64/daana-cli.sha256 before each invocation (engine.py:66) and I confirmed the committed pin actually matches the vendored binary (sha256sum -c returns OK).","subprocess.run in src/adss/destination.py:62 — argument list, no shell. notebook/shot/cache are Project paths; height is an int default; the only non-literal is str(height). env is os.environ plus PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH=<repo>/.browsers.","destination.py:99-101 — shim() takes its build number from WANTED.search(marimo's stdout+stderr), which is subprocess output and therefore not fully trusted. But the pattern is r'chromium(?:_headless_shell)?-(\\d+)' and group(1) is digits only, so the value can only produce a path segment like 'chromium-1234' inside <repo>/.browsers. No traversal, no argv, no execution — the symlink target comes from globbing /opt/pw-browsers, never from the output.","Fixture replay path traversal (src/adss/source.py:89-103) — replay() does (directory / name).read_bytes() where name comes from the manifest's 'requests' values, so a value of '../../../etc/x' would escape das/fixtures. I checked how those values are produced: record() (source.py:78-81) generates them itself as f'page-{len(requests):04d}.json' from a counter, and keys them by the locally-built page_url(). Nothing from the HTTP response — not the body, not a header, not the redirected URL — ever becomes a manifest value. Poisoning it therefore requires hand-editing a committed file under das/fixtures, which is repository content reviewed in a PR. Impact would also be limited to reading a file that happens to be valid OData JSON into the parquet lake, not execution.","urlopen host/protocol control (src/adss/source.py:52) — the URL is f'{service.rstrip(\"/\")}/{entity}?$count=true&$top=...&$skip=...' with service and entity from das/contracts/*.yaml (repo content, https://demodata.grapecity.com/...). Nothing external picks the host or scheme. I checked redirect behaviour in the interpreter here (3.11.15): HTTPRedirectHandler.http_error_302 refuses any Location whose scheme is not http/https/ftp, so an upstream redirect cannot reach file:// and cannot become a local file read. A redirect to another http host would only change where response bytes come from — no credentials or headers are attached to the request, the body is used only as data, and the manifest is still keyed by the original URL. That is host-level SSRF with no sink, on a --live code path that is off by default.","yaml.load / unsafe deserialization — every YAML read is yaml.safe_load: contract.py:131, model.py:97, uss.py:89, mapping.py:51, question.py:63, engine.py:35. No yaml.load, no eval, no exec, no pickle, no dynamic import (the only importlib use is importlib.metadata.version in __init__.py; the two function-level imports, contract.py:165 and uss.py:133, are static module names).","SQL generation from outside the repository (src/adss/das.py, src/adss/uss.py) — I traced every interpolated value. das.py takes contract.table (a *.yaml filename stem), column.source_path/target_name/sql_type, and contract.primary_keys, all from das/contracts/*.yaml. uss.py takes entity/attribute/measure ids and types from dab/model.yaml and dab/uss.yaml. calendar_sql() is a constant. checks.py:68/78/83/96 interpolate entity.id from dab/model.yaml. Not one interpolated value originates in an HTTP response body, a fixture, or a filename from outside the repo. This is the 'a malicious contract could inject SQL' case the brief calls out, and I agree it is not a finding here: those files are PR-reviewed repository content, and whoever can edit them already gets arbitrary execution from the CI job that runs pytest and `adss build` on the PR's own code.","Landed data becoming SQL identifiers (src/adss/landing.py:30-45) — the dlt resource yields a fixed key set (payload, source_system, source_entity, source_url) with payload declared as a single json-typed column, so no key from an OData record is ever promoted to a column name, a child table, or a file path. The layout string uses only dlt's own placeholders ({table_name} = contract stem, {load_id}, {file_id}, {ext}).","platform.install() (src/adss/platform.py:105-110) splitting on ';\\n' and executing each chunk — the only callers are cli.py:202 (a das/sql/*.sql file written seconds earlier in the same command from generated text) and cli.py:277 (the in-memory generated string, deliberately not the on-disk file). No external content reaches it.","sqlfluff.fix in src/adss/sqlformat.py:25 — the .sqlfluff config sets `templater = raw`, so no Jinja rendering happens over the generated SQL, and the input to formatted() is in any case generated only from repo YAML. library_path/macro loading would be config-driven, i.e. repo content.","engine.metadata_schema/is_installed (uncommitted work in src/adss/engine.py:33-52) — reads dab/connections.yaml with safe_load and passes the schema name as a bound parameter ('... WHERE table_schema = ?', [schema]), not by interpolation.","The absolute repo path embedded as a SQL string literal in raw_view_sql (das.py:47-49, read_parquet('{glob}')) — a checkout directory containing a single quote would break or inject into that statement. The path is the developer's own choice of checkout location (CI checks out under /home/runner/work/<repo>/<repo>), not something an attacker supplies, so I do not treat it as external data.","Project.discover() (project.py:21-26) walking up for pyproject.toml, which makes the engine binary and its .sha256 both come from whatever root it finds — running `adss` inside an untrusted tree would run that tree's bin/linux-amd64/daana-cli, since the pin file sits beside the binary and attests reproducibility rather than origin. That is the same exposure as running any dev tool (make, pre-commit, pytest) inside an untrusted checkout, and the brief treats CLI invocation context as trusted.","HTML injection into the marimo page (destinations/app.py:104-115) — mo.md renders raw HTML, and it interpolates a1['order_month'].min()/max(). I checked the question's uss.sql: order_month is cal.month_label, which calendar_sql() builds with strftime over a DATE, so it cannot carry markup. The one genuinely source-derived string, destination_country, reaches only n_unique() (an int), the Vega spec (SVG text), and mo.ui.table (React-escaped). Every other interpolation (q1.question, q1.persona, defined[token]) is repo YAML.","CI (.github/workflows/ci.yml) — trigger is `pull_request`, not `pull_request_target`; no secrets are referenced; ${{ }} expansions appear only in a concurrency group and a cache key, never inside a `run:` body. The 'Read the engine pin' step pipes repo file content into $GITHUB_OUTPUT via command substitution (not shell re-evaluation); a multi-line .sha256 could add extra output lines, but the only consumer is the cache key, and a poisoned cache is caught by the subsequent `sha256sum -c` before the binary is executed. source.yml touches the network but only records fixtures and diffs them; it never commits or pushes.","Note on repository