odata.nextLink` or any URL from the response, so a hostile OData service cannot redirect the fetcher at an internal host or a file:// path. urllib's default redirect handler additionally refuses non-http/https/ftp redirect targets. Endpoint.url is composed from contract YAML (service + entity), which is reviewed repository content -- a contract declaring `service: file:///...` is a repo-content change, out of scope by the stated trust model.","MALICIOUS CONTRACT / MODEL / MAPPING / QUESTION SQL INJECTING SQL OR PATHS -- explicitly considered and rejected per the stated trust model. das/contracts/*.yaml, dab/model.yaml, dab/uss.yaml, dab/mappings/*.yaml and docs/questions/**/{staged,uss}.sql are all reviewed repository content, and anyone who can change them already executes arbitrary code through the CI gate (pre-commit runs pytest). Contract-derived identifiers are interpolated unquoted into generated DDL in src/adss/das.py, and question SQL is executed verbatim in src/adss/checks.py and destinations/app.py -- both are repo-content-to-SQL, not external-data-to-SQL, so neither is a finding here. Every YAML load in the change uses yaml.safe_load (contract.py:131, model.py:97, mapping.py:51, uss.py:89, engine.py:35); none uses yaml.load or a full-constructor loader, so a YAML file cannot instantiate Python objects.","THE ENGINE'S cwd=dab/ CHANGING WHAT EXECUTES -- nothing writable from outside lands there. The engine is invoked with cwd=project.dab (src/adss/cli.py:69) and reads workflow.yaml, connections.yaml, model.yaml and mappings/*.yaml from it. connections.yaml declares only `type: duckdb` and `database: ../warehouse.duckdb` -- no extension repository, no plugin path, no hook command, nothing that names an executable or a fetch. No code in src/adss writes into dab/; the only files created there (model.json, workflow.compiled.json, mappings/*.json, logs/) are the engine's own output and are gitignored, so they are regenerated from the reviewed YAML on every CI run rather than carried in. External data reaches the engine only through the warehouse's das__staged views, as row values.","pre-commit HOOKS AS A FETCH-AND-EXECUTE SURFACE -- there is no fetch. Every hook is `repo: local` with `language: system` and an entry of the form `uv run --frozen <tool>`, so pre-commit clones and executes nothing from a remote tag; the tool versions are fixed by uv.lock. pre-commit passes filenames as argv rather than through a shell, so there is no word-splitting or command injection from a filename; a file named like a flag would need to be committed, which is repo content. The one hook worth naming is `pytest`, which means the CI gate job executes the PR's Python -- but that is inherent to running a test suite on a PR and is bounded by the `pull_request` event's read-only token and absent secrets (below).","UNTRUSTED PR CONTENT RUNNING WITH ELEVATED PRIVILEGE -- not present. grep over .github/workflows finds no `pull_request_target`, no `workflow_run`, no `issue_comment`, no `secrets.*` reference and no `environment:`. Triggers are `push: branches: ['**']`, `pull_request`, `schedule` and `workflow_dispatch` only. A fork `pull_request` run gets a read-only GITHUB_TOKEN and no secrets by default, so the arbitrary code it runs (pytest, `adss build`, the vendored binary) has nothing to steal or push to; a same-repo push already implies write access. There is no flow where fork content and repository credentials meet.","MUTABLE ACTION TAGS (actions/checkout@v5, actions/cache@v4, astral-sh/setup-uv@v7) -- real supply-chain exposure in principle, classified as missing hardening and excluded by the brief. Pinning to a commit SHA would be the standard mitigation; nothing about this change makes the exposure worse or concretely exploitable, since these jobs hold no secrets and a read-only token on the only untrusted-input path.","DEPENDENCY RESOLUTION -- uv.lock has 1729 hash entries, every `source` is `registry = \"https://pypi.org/simple\"` (plus 