{
  "reproduced": "adr-0002-is-wrong",
  "headline": "ADR 0002's mechanism is right and its conclusion is right, but the stated consequence is WRONG: the relationship view does rank pairs by effective timestamp and keep only open rows, but the rank is PARTITION BY <src>_key, type_key, so a changed foreign key never drops the child out of the view \u2014 it silently re-points every one of that child's rows at the NEW parent and erases the old pair. Verified: after CH1 moved P1->P2, the pair table held BOTH rows (both row_st='Y') and the view held CH1|P2 only. The real reason to read the pair table is history, not disappearance. Separately, ADR 0002's supporting claim that \"view_<entity>_with_rel carries no relationship key columns at all\" is only true on the relationship's SOURCE side; on the TARGET side it carries the source entity's key AND all its attributes, fanned out \u2014 sum(CHILD_AMOUNT) over view_PARENT_with_rel was 200.75 against a true 150.75.",
  "objects": [
    "dab.CHILD_PARENT_x \u2014 BASE TABLE. The pair table. Named <SRC>_<TGT>_x from the entity pair, NOT from the relationship name; a second relationship between the same two entities would land in this same table, distinguished only by type_key.",
    "dab.view_CHILD_BELONGS_TO_PARENT \u2014 VIEW, named view_<SRC>_<NAME>_<TGT>. Body is literally `SELECT * FROM dab.f_child_parent_x();` \u2014 no type_key predicate.",
    "dab.v_CHILD_BELONGS_TO_PARENT \u2014 VIEW, named v_<SRC>_<NAME>_<TGT>. The raw pair table INNER JOINed to dab__meta.atom_contx_desc to add a rel_name column; no ranking, no row_st filter.",
    "dab.f_CHILD_PARENT_x \u2014 DuckDB TABLE MACRO (invisible to information_schema.tables; only duckdb_functions() shows it). Takes p_eff_tmstp with a default, so it is point-in-time capable: f_child_parent_x(TIMESTAMP '2026-01-15') returned CH1|P1 while f_child_parent_x() returned CH1|P2.",
    "dab.f_PARENT_WITH_REL \u2014 TABLE MACRO that a relationship MUTATES rather than creates. With no relationship it is a passthrough (verified against the repo's slice-1 warehouse: f_ORDER_WITH_REL is `SELECT f_ORDER.ORDER_key, ... FROM dab.f_order(p_eff_tmstp)`). Once the entity is a relationship TARGET it becomes a double LEFT JOIN through the pair macro to the source entity, so view_PARENT_with_rel gains CHILD_key, CHILD_CODE, CHILD_AMOUNT, CHILD_PLACED_ON and fans out.",
    "dab.f_CHILD_WITH_REL \u2014 TABLE MACRO, unchanged passthrough. The relationship's SOURCE side gains nothing; view_CHILD_with_rel is byte-identical to view_CHILD.",
    "dab__meta.atom_contx_desc \u2014 gains one row, ('1','CHILD_BELONGS_TO_PARENT'), mapping the relationship id to the type_key used in the pair table. atom_contx_key is VARCHAR, type_key is BIGINT, so a lookup needs CAST (the engine's own v_* view does exactly that).",
    "NOT created by a relationship: the dab__stage relx2_load/relx2_reader/relx2_delta/relx2_integration/relx2_target staging tables already exist in the repo's relationship-free slice-1 warehouse \u2014 install/deploy makes them regardless.",
    "Baseline for the diff: slice 1 (one entity, no relationship) has exactly BASE TABLE ORDER_desc/ORDER_focal/ORDER_idfr, VIEW v_ORDER_desc/view_ORDER/view_ORDER_hist/view_ORDER_with_rel, MACRO f_ORDER/f_ORDER_WITH_REL. My two-entity+one-relationship model added exactly 4 dab objects on top of the per-entity set: CHILD_PARENT_x, view_CHILD_BELONGS_TO_PARENT, v_CHILD_BELONGS_TO_PARENT, f_CHILD_PARENT_x."
  ],
  "pairTable": "DESCRIBE dab.\"CHILD_PARENT_x\"\n  CHILD_key                VARCHAR\n  PARENT_key               VARCHAR\n  type_key                 BIGINT\n  eff_tmstp                TIMESTAMP\n  ver_tmstp                TIMESTAMP\n  row_st                   VARCHAR\n  data_key                 BIGINT\n  inst_key                 BIGINT\n  inst_row_key             BIGINT\n  popln_tmstp              TIMESTAMP\n\nThere is NO sta_tmstp / end_tmstp on the pair table (the entity _desc table has those; the pair table does not).\n\nSELECT * FROM dab.\"CHILD_PARENT_x\"  -- final state of the probe, 7 rows\nCHILD_key | PARENT_key | type_key | eff_tmstp | ver_tmstp | row_st | data_key | inst_key | inst_row_key | popln_tmstp\nCH1 | P1 | 1 | 2026-01-01 00:00:00 | 2026-09-07 22:25:15.586568 | Y | 6714190177621610116 | 4264648631195359398 | 8905999647301224081 | 2026-09-07 22:25:15.586568\nCH1 | P2 | 1 | 2026-02-01 00:00:00 | 2026-09-07 22:26:33.824411 | Y | 6714190177621610116 | -6074331792173279437 | 8905999647301224081 | 2026-09-07 22:26:33.824411\nCH2 | P2 | 1 | 2026-01-01 00:00:00 | 2026-09-07 22:25:15.586568 | Y | 6714190177621610116 | 4264648631195359398 | 8905999647301224081 | 2026-09-07 22:25:15.586568\nCH3 | P3 | 1 | 2026-03-01 00:00:00 | 2026-09-07 22:27:20.904874 | Y | 6714190177621610116 | -3316671293312042104 | 8905999647301224081 | 2026-09-07 22:27:20.904874\nCH4 | P1 | 1 | 2026-01-01 00:00:00 | 2026-09-07 22:25:15.586568 | Y | 6714190177621610116 | 4264648631195359398 | 8905999647301224081 | 2026-09-07 22:25:15.586568\nCH5 | P1 | 1 | 2026-04-01 00:00:00 | 2026-09-07 22:27:42.891238 | Y | 6714190177621610116 | 5702846401563624914 | 8905999647301224081 | 2026-09-07 22:27:42.891238\nCH5 | P2 | 1 | 2026-04-01 00:00:00 | 2026-09-07 22:27:42.891238 | Y | 6714190177621610116 | 5702846401563624914 | 8905999647301224081 | 2026-09-07 22:27:42.891238\n\n(CH6 \u2014 a child that never had a parent \u2014 has no row at all. CH5 has TWO rows at the SAME eff_tmstp because its source held two parent codes in one load; nothing in the engine forbids that.)",
  "relationshipView": "The real name is dab.view_CHILD_BELONGS_TO_PARENT \u2014 i.e. view_<SOURCE>_<RELATIONSHIP NAME>_<TARGET>, with the model's casing preserved. There is a SECOND view, v_CHILD_BELONGS_TO_PARENT (v_ prefix), which is a different object.\n\nCREATE VIEW dab.view_CHILD_BELONGS_TO_PARENT AS SELECT * FROM dab.f_child_parent_x();\n\nDESCRIBE dab.\"view_CHILD_BELONGS_TO_PARENT\"\n  CHILD_key                VARCHAR\n  PARENT_key               VARCHAR\n  type_key                 BIGINT\n  eff_tmstp                TIMESTAMP\n  ver_tmstp                TIMESTAMP\n\nSELECT * FROM dab.\"view_CHILD_BELONGS_TO_PARENT\"  -- 6 rows, final state\nCHILD_key | PARENT_key | type_key | eff_tmstp | ver_tmstp\nCH1 | P2 | 1 | 2026-02-01 00:00:00 | 2026-09-07 22:26:33.824411\nCH2 | P2 | 1 | 2026-01-01 00:00:00 | 2026-09-07 22:25:15.586568\nCH3 | P3 | 1 | 2026-03-01 00:00:00 | 2026-09-07 22:27:20.904874\nCH4 | P1 | 1 | 2026-01-01 00:00:00 | 2026-09-07 22:25:15.586568\nCH5 | P1 | 1 | 2026-04-01 00:00:00 | 2026-09-07 22:27:42.891238\nCH5 | P2 | 1 | 2026-04-01 00:00:00 | 2026-09-07 22:27:42.891238\n\nThe macro behind it, verbatim from duckdb_functions().macro_definition \u2014 this is the ADR's \"ranks pairs and keeps the open rows\", and it is exactly right about the mechanism:\n\nSELECT CHILD_key, PARENT_key, type_key, eff_tmstp, ver_tmstp\nFROM (SELECT CHILD_key, PARENT_key, type_key, eff_tmstp, ver_tmstp, row_st,\n             rank() OVER (PARTITION BY CHILD_key, type_key\n                          ORDER BY eff_tmstp DESC, ver_tmstp DESC) AS nbr\n      FROM dab.CHILD_PARENT_x\n      WHERE (eff_tmstp <= CAST(p_eff_tmstp AS TIMESTAMP))) AS A\nWHERE ((nbr = 1) AND (row_st = 'Y'))\n\nThe other view, dab.v_CHILD_BELONGS_TO_PARENT, is the RAW pair table plus a name \u2014 no rank, no row_st filter:\nCREATE VIEW dab.v_CHILD_BELONGS_TO_PARENT AS SELECT an.CHILD_key, an.PARENT_key, bn.val_str AS rel_name, an.eff_tmstp, an.ver_tmstp, an.row_st, an.data_key, an.inst_key, an.inst_row_key, an.popln_tmstp FROM dab.CHILD_PARENT_x AS an INNER JOIN dab__meta.atom_contx_desc AS bn ON ((an.type_key = CAST(bn.atom_contx_key AS BIGINT)));\nDESCRIBE gives: CHILD_key VARCHAR | PARENT_key VARCHAR | rel_name VARCHAR | eff_tmstp TIMESTAMP | ver_tmstp TIMESTAMP | row_st VARCHAR | data_key BIGINT | inst_key BIGINT | inst_row_key BIGINT | popln_tmstp TIMESTAMP\nand it returned all 4 pair rows (including both CH1 rows) at the point where the ranked view returned 3.",
  "changedForeignKey": "TEST: CH1 loaded pointing at P1 with extracted_at 2026-01-01. Then a second das__staged row for CH1 pointing at P2 with extracted_at 2026-02-01 (append, FULL_LOG \u2014 exactly how DAS lands a change). `daana-cli execute` re-run, exit 0.\n\nBEFORE \u2014 pair TABLE dab.CHILD_PARENT_x, 3 rows:\nCH1 | P1 | 1 | 2026-01-01 00:00:00 | 2026-09-07 22:25:15.586568 | Y\nCH2 | P2 | 1 | 2026-01-01 00:00:00 | 2026-09-07 22:25:15.586568 | Y\nCH4 | P1 | 1 | 2026-01-01 00:00:00 | 2026-09-07 22:25:15.586568 | Y\nBEFORE \u2014 relationship VIEW, the same 3 rows.\n\nAFTER \u2014 pair TABLE dab.CHILD_PARENT_x, 4 rows, BOTH CH1 rows present, BOTH row_st='Y':\nCH1 | P1 | 1 | 2026-01-01 00:00:00 | 2026-09-07 22:25:15.586568 | Y\nCH1 | P2 | 1 | 2026-02-01 00:00:00 | 2026-09-07 22:26:33.824411 | Y\nCH2 | P2 | 1 | 2026-01-01 00:00:00 | 2026-09-07 22:25:15.586568 | Y\nCH4 | P1 | 1 | 2026-01-01 00:00:00 | 2026-09-07 22:25:15.586568 | Y\n\nAFTER \u2014 
